<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarai was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> in the 1240s near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>; a second capital, New Sarai, was built further upstream in the 14th century. Rus' princes travelled there to receive their patents to rule, and it grew into a large trading city with mosques, workshops and merchants' quarters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacked it in 1395, and the Crimean khan destroyed what remained in 1502.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Batu built his capital, <b>Sarai</b>, on the lower Volga, and Rus' princes travelled there, and sometimes on to Karakorum, to receive patents of authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1243 the Mongols decided who held the grand-princely title, by issuing a patent, the yarlyk, to the prince they favoured, and claimants travelled to the Horde at <b>Sarai</b>, and sometimes to Karakorum, to receive it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Bolghar recovered and became one of the Horde’s important cities, where coins were minted and where Batu appears to have kept a seat before <b>Sarai</b> was built.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Timur then sacked the Horde's cities, including <b>Sarai</b>, a blow from which it never fully recovered.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The western or right wing, ruled by Batu from <b>Sarai</b> on the lower Volga, is often called the Blue Horde, though medieval sources use the colour names inconsistently.</blockquote><a href="/state/blue-horde">Blue Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>The rump around <b>Sarai</b>, ruled by Küchük Muhammad and his sons, became known as the Great Horde, claiming to be the senior Jochid state.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-horde">Great Horde</a></li></ul></section>
